Whatever ’tis, whose beauty here below,  
Doth in a flower, or running crystal, show;  
Whose hand in every leaf doth write a line,  
Too small for pride, yet legible to thine;  
Teach me to read, in all things that I see,  
The silent motions of thy grace and thee.

The stars, like tapers in thy temple, move,  
And burn obedience in their orbs above;  
The winds, though none behold their secret wing,  
Make all the fields thy hidden praises sing;  
The rivers haste, yet murmur as they go,  
That all return to thee from whom they flow.

The sun doth rise, and with a golden rod  
Awakes the world to labour, and to God;  
The moon, pale pilgrim, walking in the night,  
Confesseth borrowed beams, and lesser light;  
So doth my soul, when thou art pleased to shine,  
Grow bright, yet owns the brightness none of mine.

The seed, interr’d in darkness and in clay,  
Finds out a resurrection into day;  
The bare boughs, dead to sense, in spring receive  
A power unseen, commanding them to live;  
Thus under grief, when comforts all depart,  
Thy buried mercy quickens in my heart.

No creature stirs, but in its course doth tell  
Some inward spring, which maketh all things well;  
No wave can lift, no little bird can fall,  
But thou art secretly the life of all.  
O Thou, whose grace through visible motions flows,  
Be seen in me, as in the rose that grows.
